\subsection{Problem Formulation}

We consider \emph{recursive synthetic training}: a sequence of models $M_0, M_1, \ldots, M_T$ where each $M_t$ is trained on a mixture of real data and synthetic data generated by $M_{t-1}$.
Let $r_t \in [0,1]$ denote the fraction of real data at generation $t$.
Given a fixed total real-data budget $B = T \cdot r_{\text{base}}$ across $T$ generations, we seek an allocation $\{r_1, \ldots, r_T\}$ satisfying $\sum_t r_t = B$ that minimizes cumulative model degradation.

The core challenge is \emph{temporal}: uniform allocation wastes real data on healthy generations while under-investing in critical collapse phases.
We propose a closed-loop controller that monitors spectral health online and redistributes budget toward generations exhibiting early signs of representation collapse.

\subsection{Spectral Health Signal}

We adopt the \emph{effective rank} (RankMe)~\cite{rankme2023} of the output representation Gram matrix as our collapse indicator.
At each generation $t$, we perform a forward pass of $M_t$ on a held-out probe set of $N$ examples, extract last-layer hidden states, and mean-pool to obtain a matrix $\mathbf{Z} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times D}$.
We compute the SVD of $\mathbf{Z}$ to obtain singular values $\sigma_1 \geq \sigma_2 \geq \cdots \geq \sigma_{\min(N,D)}$, normalize them as $p_i = \sigma_i / \sum_j \sigma_j$, and define:
\begin{equation}
  S_t = \rho(\mathbf{Z}) = \exp\!\left(-\sum_i p_i \log p_i\right)
\end{equation}
The effective rank $S_t$ measures the diversity of learned representations: collapse concentrates the singular spectrum, causing $S_t$ to decrease.
This signal is cheap to compute (one forward pass, no gradient) and prior work shows that calibration degradation (ECE) precedes accuracy loss by 1--2 iterations~\cite{calibration2025}; whether spectral signals exhibit similar or earlier lead time is an empirical question we investigate.

\subsection{Proportional Controller with Budget Constraint}

We smooth the raw signal with an exponential moving average:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{S}_t = \alpha \cdot S_t + (1 - \alpha) \cdot \tilde{S}_{t-1}, \quad \alpha = 0.5
\end{equation}
and define the normalized decline rate:
\begin{equation}
  \Delta_t = \frac{\tilde{S}_{t-1} - \tilde{S}_t}{|\tilde{S}_0|}
\end{equation}

The controller computes a raw target ratio for the next generation:
\begin{equation}
  r_{t+1}^{\text{raw}} = \underbrace{\frac{B_{\text{rem}}}{T - t}}_{\text{uniform residual}} + \underbrace{k \cdot \max(0,\; \Delta_t - \tau)}_{\text{reactive boost}}
\end{equation}
where $B_{\text{rem}} = B - \sum_{i=1}^{t} r_i$ is the remaining budget, $\tau$ is a dead-zone threshold below which no correction is applied, and $k$ is the proportional gain.

Budget feasibility is enforced by clipping:
\begin{equation}
  r_{t+1} = \text{clip}\!\left(r_{t+1}^{\text{raw}},\; r_{\min},\; \min\!\left(r_{\max},\; B_{\text{rem}} - (T - t - 1) \cdot r_{\min}\right)\right)
\end{equation}
guaranteeing that every future generation receives at least $r_{\min}$.
The first generation uses $r_1 = r_{\text{base}}$ (no prior signal).

Controller hyperparameters: threshold $\tau$, gain $k$, EMA coefficient $\alpha$, bounds $r_{\min}$ and $r_{\max}$.

\subsection{Experimental Design}

We compare three conditions sharing an identical total real-data budget $B$:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Pure synthetic} ($r_t = 0\;\forall t$): collapse reference establishing degradation ceiling.
  \item \textbf{Fixed uniform} ($r_t = r_{\text{base}}\;\forall t$): static baseline representing naive budget-spreading.
  \item \textbf{Spectral-guided adaptive} (ours): closed-loop allocation via the controller above.
\end{enumerate}

All conditions use the same base model, training hyperparameters, and synthetic generation procedure, differing only in real-data scheduling.
We evaluate on: effective rank $\rho$, held-out perplexity, lexical diversity (distinct-4), and HellaSwag accuracy (acc\_norm).
